<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter IV of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1319–<a href="/years/1387">1387</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Peter the Ceremonious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter IV, called the Ceremonious, was king of Aragon from 1336. He annexed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1343-1344, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the War of the Two Peters and suppressed the noble Unions. In 1379-1380 the Catalan duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypati">Neopatras</a> accepted his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Peter IV of Aragon" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaume Mateu (Before 1402 - After 1452) Born in Documented in Valencia. Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaume_Mateu_-_Peter_IV_the_Ceremonious_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter IV (Catalan: Pere IV d'Aragó) was born on 5 September 1319 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a>, the eldest son of Alfons IV, then Count of Urgell, and his first wife, Teresa d'Entença. He inherited all his father's titles except Urgell, which went to his younger brother James. On succeeding he called a corts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> for his coronation and crowned himself, disappointing the Archbishop of Zaragoza and rejecting the surrender Peter II had made to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. He then stopped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> to affirm the Usatges and Constitutions of Catalonia, which offended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, where the ceremony had usually been performed. In 1338 he married Maria, daughter of Philip III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-ii-of-navarre">Joan II of Navarre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Peter was from 1336 king of Aragon, Sardinia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. His reign was occupied with strengthening the crown against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-aragon">Union of Aragon</a> and other devices of the nobility, and with wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, the Mezzogiorno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the Balearics. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> from his brother-in-law James III after declaring him a contumacious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in February 1343. His fleet landed in May, James's army was defeated at the Battle of Santa Ponça, and Peter had himself crowned king of Majorca in 1344.</p><p>His quarrels with the Union were violent. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> he was held under guard by the Union, and he and his queen were forced to dance with the common folk to show his subservience. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> struck Valencia in May 1348 and enabled him to escape. At the Battle of Epila on 21 July 1348 he defeated the unionist forces, executed thirteen Union leaders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and had the bell that summoned the Valencian Union melted and poured down the throats of its leaders. In 1356 he began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-two-peters">War of the Two Peters</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter I of Castile</a>, which ended in 1375 with the Treaty of Almazan and no winner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Peter's wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> made him Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Neopatria. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-courts">Catalan Courts</a> of 1358–1359 he instituted the Deputation of the General, or Generalitat, whose first president was Berenguer de Cruïlles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a>. Around 1370 he ordered the compilation of the Chronicle of Sant Joan de la Penya to record the historical basis of the crown's authority. In 1350, in response to the plague, he ordered the mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> to build a new Jewish cemetery. He had frequent conflicts with the inquisitor general of Aragon, Nicolas Eymerich. Peter died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, aged 67, on 6 January 1387, leaving sons by his third marriage, among them John I and Martin I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter IV of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Peter IV</b> fought the long War of the Two Peters against Castile from 1356 to 1369, in which Aragon suffered repeated invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…France and the papacy over Sicily, nobles and towns organised in the Union of Aragon forced him to grant the General Privilege, and in 1287 Alfonso III yielded the still wider Privileges of the Union, until <b>Peter IV</b> crushed the Union at the Battle of Épila in 1348.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1359, at the Corts of Cervera, <b>Peter IV</b> accepted a standing committee to collect the taxes voted by the Corts, the Diputació del General, later called the Generalitat, which grew into a powerful body defending the Principality’s constitutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1379-1380 the duchies passed to <b>Peter IV of Aragon</b>, who called the Acropolis the most beautiful jewel in the world.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-neopatras">Duchy of Neopatras</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Peter IV of Aragon</b> seized the kingdom in 1343-1344, and James III was killed at Llucmajor in 1349 trying to retake it.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.79,0.81&amp;year=1363" aria-label="Balaguer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Peter IV of Aragon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5051 3999h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5051 3999h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.79,0.81&amp;year=1363">What was here in 1363? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
